(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-02"] = {"4.1":{"s":"Atoms are made of three subatomic particles. The electron, discovered by J. J. Thomson, is negatively charged and very light. The proton, identified by Rutherford, is positively charged and about 1800 times heavier than the electron. The neutron, discovered by Chadwick, has no charge and nearly the same mass as the proton. Protons and neutrons are in the nucleus; electrons move around it.","w":["The neutron is neutral, so it is the only one of the three that a magnetic or electric field cannot deflect."],"x":["An atom has three kinds of subatomic particles. The electron was found by J. J. Thomson in 1897. It has a charge of −1 (−1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C) and a very small mass, 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg. The proton was identified by Rutherford. It has a charge of +1 and a mass of about 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg, roughly 1836 times that of the electron. The neutron was discovered by Chadwick in 1932. It has no charge and a mass slightly more than that of the proton.","Protons and neutrons are in the small, dense nucleus, and are called nucleons. Electrons move in the space around the nucleus. Almost all the mass of an atom is in the nucleus, and almost all the volume is the empty space where the electrons are.","An electric or magnetic field bends a beam of electrons and protons, in opposite directions, but not a beam of neutrons, because they have no charge. Common mistake: saying that the mass of the electron is nothing. It is small, but it is not zero."],"e":"Compare the masses: the mass of a proton divided by the mass of an electron is 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ / 9.1 × 10⁻³¹, which is about 1836. So an atom's mass is nearly all in the nucleus."},"4.2":{"s":"The atomic number Z is the number of protons in the nucleus; it is also the number of electrons in a neutral atom, and it identifies the element. The mass number A is the number of protons plus neutrons, the number of nucleons. The number of neutrons is therefore A minus Z. An atom is written with A as a superscript and Z as a subscript before the symbol.","f":[{"x":"N = A - Z","n":"Number of neutrons N from the mass number A and the atomic number Z.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.2, p.36"}],"w":["A cation has fewer electrons than protons and an anion more, but Z does not change."],"x":["The atomic number Z is the number of protons in the nucleus of an atom. It identifies the element: every atom with Z = 6 is carbon. In a neutral atom the number of electrons is also Z.","The mass number A is the number of protons plus neutrons, that is, the number of nucleons. The number of neutrons is N = A − Z. An atom of an element X is written as ᴬ_Z X, with A at the top left and Z at the bottom left. For example, ²³₁₁Na has 11 protons, 11 electrons and 12 neutrons.","For ions the number of electrons changes, but Z and A do not. A cation has lost electrons, so it has fewer electrons than protons: Na⁺ has 11 protons and 10 electrons. An anion has gained electrons: Cl⁻ has 17 protons and 18 electrons.","Common mistake: using the atomic mass in u (like 35.5) as the mass number. The mass number is always a whole number."],"e":"Find the particles in ³⁵₁₇Cl⁻. Protons: 17. Neutrons: 35 − 17 = 18. Electrons: 17 + 1 = 18, because of the negative charge."},"4.3":{"s":"Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same atomic number but different mass numbers, because they have different numbers of neutrons. Isobars have the same mass number but different atomic numbers. Isotones have the same number of neutrons but different atomic and mass numbers. Isotopes of an element have the same chemical properties but slightly different physical properties.","w":["Isotopes: same Z. Isobars: same A. Isotones: same number of neutrons."],"x":["Isotopes are atoms of the same element, so they have the same atomic number, but they have different mass numbers because they have different numbers of neutrons. Hydrogen has three isotopes: protium (no neutron), deuterium (1 neutron) and tritium (2 neutrons). Isotopes have the same chemical properties, since they have the same electrons, but slightly different physical properties, such as density and boiling point.","Isobars are atoms of different elements with the same mass number, for example ⁴⁰₁₈Ar and ⁴⁰₂₀Ca. They have different atomic numbers and so different chemical properties.","Isotones are atoms of different elements with the same number of neutrons, for example ³⁷₁₇Cl and ³⁹₁₉K, which both have 20 neutrons.","A quick memory aid: isotopes have the same Z, isobars have the same A, and isotones have the same N. Common mistake: mixing isobars and isotopes. To decide, compare Z and A, and then find N = A − Z."],"e":"Classify ¹²₆C and ¹⁴₆C: same Z, different A, so they are isotopes. ¹⁴₆C and ¹⁴₇N: same A, different Z, so they are isobars."},"4.4":{"s":"Rutherford's model has a small, heavy, positive nucleus with electrons moving round it. Its first drawback: by Maxwell's theory, a charged particle in orbit is accelerated and must radiate energy, so the electron would spiral into the nucleus and the atom would collapse. The second: the model does not describe how the electrons are arranged or what their energies are.","w":["Rutherford's model explains the nucleus but not why the atom is stable. An orbiting electron should radiate and fall in.","It also cannot explain the line spectrum of hydrogen."],"x":["Rutherford's gold foil experiment showed that an atom has a very small, dense, positively charged nucleus, with the electrons moving round it, like planets round a sun. Most alpha particles went straight through the foil, a few were deflected, and a very few bounced back.","The model has two serious drawbacks. First, by Maxwell's theory of electromagnetism, a charged particle moving in a circle is accelerating, and must give out radiation and lose energy. The electron would then spiral into the nucleus in a very short time, and the atom would collapse. But atoms are stable.","Second, the model says nothing about how the electrons are arranged round the nucleus, or about their energies. It cannot explain the line spectra of atoms, which show that electrons have only certain energies.","These faults led Bohr to put forward a new model in 1913, based on the ideas of quantised energy."],"e":"If Rutherford's model were right, a hydrogen atom would last only about 10⁻⁸ seconds, as its electron would radiate and fall into the nucleus. This is clearly not true, so the model is incomplete."},"4.5":{"s":"Two developments led to Bohr's model. Light behaves as a wave (with wavelength, frequency and speed) and also as a stream of photons, each with energy proportional to its frequency. Hydrogen gas heated or excited gives a line emission spectrum, not a continuous one. The lines fall in series: Lyman (ultraviolet), Balmer (visible), Paschen, Brackett and Pfund (infrared). Balmer and Rydberg found a formula for their wavenumbers.","f":[{"x":"E = h*nu = h*c/lambda","n":"Energy of a photon: frequency nu, wavelength lambda, speed of light c, Planck's constant h.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.5, p.39"},{"x":"nu_bar = R_H*(1/n_1^2 - 1/n_2^2)","n":"Rydberg formula for the wavenumber of the hydrogen lines; R_H = 109677 per cm; n_2 > n_1. The Balmer series has n_1 = 2.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.5, p.40"}],"w":["Shorter wavelength means higher frequency and higher energy: blue light carries more energy per photon than red.","Series: Lyman n₁ = 1 (UV), Balmer 2 (visible), Paschen 3, Brackett 4, Pfund 5 (all infrared)."],"x":["Light behaves as a wave, with wavelength λ, frequency ν and speed c = νλ (3 × 10⁸ m/s in vacuum). It also behaves as a stream of particles called photons. The energy of a photon is E = hν = hc/λ, where h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s. So a photon of shorter wavelength has more energy: blue light has more energy than red light.","A hot solid gives a continuous spectrum, but an excited gas gives a line spectrum, with only certain wavelengths. Each element has its own line spectrum, so it works like a fingerprint. The hydrogen spectrum has several series of lines: Lyman (ultraviolet, ending at n = 1), Balmer (visible, n = 2), Paschen (infrared, n = 3), Brackett (infrared, n = 4) and Pfund (infrared, n = 5).","The Rydberg formula gives the wavenumber of the lines: ν̄ = R_H (1/n₁² − 1/n₂²), where n₂ is greater than n₁ and R_H = 109677 per cm. The wavenumber is 1/λ. For the Balmer series n₁ = 2 and n₂ = 3, 4, 5, and so on.","Common mistakes: forgetting to change nm to m in E = hc/λ, and giving the wrong end level for a series."],"e":"Find the energy of a photon of wavelength 500 nm. E = hc/λ = (6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸)/(500 × 10⁻⁹) = 3.98 × 10⁻¹⁹ J."},"4.6":{"s":"Bohr's model for hydrogen has electrons moving only in certain circular orbits, the stationary states, without radiating. Energy is emitted or absorbed only when an electron jumps between orbits, and the photon energy equals the difference. The energy and radius depend on the principal quantum number n. The energy becomes less negative as n rises, and the radius grows as n². It works for one-electron species such as H, He⁺ and Li²⁺ only.","f":[{"x":"E_n = -2.18*10^-18*(Z^2/n^2) J","n":"Energy of the electron in the n-th orbit of a hydrogen-like species of atomic number Z.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.6, p.42"},{"x":"r_n = 52.9*n^2/Z pm","n":"Radius of the n-th orbit.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.6, p.42"},{"x":"Delta E = E_f - E_i = R_H*(1/n_i^2 - 1/n_f^2)","n":"Energy change when the electron jumps from orbit n_i to n_f.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.6, p.42"}],"w":["Bohr's model works only for one-electron species: H, He⁺, Li²⁺.","Energy is negative and rises towards zero as n increases; n = 1 is the most stable level."],"x":["Bohr's model of the hydrogen atom has three ideas. The electron moves in certain circular orbits, called stationary states, and does not radiate energy while in one. Each orbit has a fixed energy and a fixed angular momentum, which is a whole-number multiple of h/2π. Energy is emitted or absorbed only when the electron jumps from one orbit to another, and the photon's energy equals the difference between the two orbit energies.","The results for a one-electron species of atomic number Z: the energy of the n-th orbit is E_n = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ Z²/n² J, and the radius is r_n = 52.9 n²/Z pm. The energy is negative, and it gets closer to zero as n rises. The lowest level, n = 1, is the ground state. The energy change in a jump is ΔE = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ Z² (1/n_i² − 1/n_f²) J.","How to use it: for a jump from n = 3 to n = 2 in hydrogen, ΔE = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ (1/4 − 1/9) = 3.03 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. This is emitted as a photon in the Balmer series, with λ = hc/ΔE, about 656 nm. The ionisation energy of hydrogen is the energy to go from n = 1 to infinity: 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J.","Limits: the model works only for H, He⁺, Li²⁺ and other one-electron species, and it cannot explain the finer lines or atoms with more electrons."],"e":"Radius of the second orbit of Li²⁺ (Z = 3): r₂ = 52.9 × 4/3 = 70.5 pm. Energy of the first orbit of He⁺ (Z = 2): E₁ = −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ × 4 = −8.72 × 10⁻¹⁸ J."},"4.7":{"s":"Bohr's model failed for bigger atoms, and it ignored two ideas: de Broglie's dual nature of matter and Heisenberg's uncertainty principle. De Broglie said that a particle has a wavelength. Heisenberg said we cannot know exactly both the position and the momentum of an electron at the same time. Quantum mechanics replaced orbits by orbitals, regions of high probability, found from the Schrödinger equation. Electrons fill orbitals by the Aufbau, Pauli and Hund rules.","f":[{"x":"lambda = h/(m*v) = h/p","n":"de Broglie wavelength of a particle of mass m and speed v.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.7, p.44"},{"x":"Delta x * Delta p >= h/(4*pi)","n":"Heisenberg uncertainty principle: position and momentum cannot both be known exactly.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 4.7, p.44"}],"w":["An orbital is a region of probability, not a path; the Bohr orbit and the orbital are different things.","Pauli: no two electrons in an atom have the same four quantum numbers (an orbital holds at most two, with opposite spins). Hund: fill degenerate orbitals singly first."],"x":["Bohr's model could not explain atoms with more than one electron. Two new ideas helped. De Broglie said that every moving particle has a wavelength, λ = h/mv = h/p. This is only noticeable for very small particles like electrons. Heisenberg's uncertainty principle says that the position and the momentum of a small particle cannot both be known exactly at the same time: Δx · Δp is at least h/4π.","Quantum mechanics, based on the Schrödinger equation, replaced orbits by orbitals. An orbital is a region of space around the nucleus in which the electron is most likely to be found. It is not a path. Each orbital is described by quantum numbers: n (the size and energy), l (the shape: s, p, d, f), m (the orientation) and s (the spin of the electron, +1/2 or −1/2).","Rules for filling the orbitals. Aufbau principle: electrons go into the orbitals of lowest energy first. Pauli exclusion principle: every electron in an atom has its own set of the four quantum numbers. So an orbital can hold two electrons at most, and they must spin in opposite ways. Hund's rule: orbitals of equal energy are filled singly first, with parallel spins, before any is doubled up.","Common mistake: describing an orbital as a circular path. An orbital is a probability region."],"e":"Electron configuration of nitrogen (Z = 7): 1s² 2s² 2p³. By Hund's rule, the three 2p electrons go into the three different 2p orbitals, each with the same spin, and not two into one orbital."}};
